# Lesson 4: Insecure Cloud Configuration

## Part 1) Goal and Vulnerability Summary

This lesson demonstrates an insecure cloud configuration in the DVSA application where an S3 bucket allows unintended file uploads. These uploaded files are automatically processed by a backend AWS Lambda function. This creates a risk where attacker-controlled data can enter the backend processing pipeline.

The affected components include:
- Amazon S3 (receipts bucket)
- AWS Lambda (DVSA-SEND-RECEIPT-EMAIL)

The security impact is that an attacker can inject arbitrary files into the system, which are then trusted and processed by backend services.

---

## Part 2) Why This Works / Root Cause

The vulnerability exists due to:

- Overly permissive S3 bucket configuration
- Lack of strict validation of uploaded objects
- Trust in S3 event-triggered data by backend Lambda functions

The system assumes that any `.raw` file in the bucket is legitimate, without verifying its origin or structure.

---

## Part 3) Environment and Setup

- DVSA deployed in AWS (us-east-1)
- AWS CLI configured
- Test file created locally
- Identified resources:
  - S3 bucket: `dvsa-receipts-bucket-446483465359-us-east-1`
  - Lambda function: `DVSA-SEND-RECEIPT-EMAIL`
