## Part 4) Reproduction Steps

### Step 1: Create a test file

```bash
echo "lesson4-test" > test.raw

Step 2: Upload file to S3 bucket
aws s3 cp test.raw \
s3://dvsa-receipts-bucket-446483465359-us-east-1/2026/04/15/b499ea14-f30f-4a5c-86ff-c3d8c53be680_b499ea14-f30f-4a5c-86ff-c3d8c53be680.raw

Step 3: Verify upload success

The upload completes successfully, indicating that the bucket accepts arbitrary file uploads.

Step 4: Check Lambda execution
Open AWS CloudWatch
Navigate to /aws/lambda/DVSA-SEND-RECEIPT-EMAIL
Open latest log stream

A new log entry appears immediately after upload, confirming that the Lambda function was triggered.

Part 5) Evidence and Proof
Successful AWS CLI upload output
S3 bucket contents showing uploaded file
Event notification configuration linking S3 to Lambda
CloudWatch logs showing new Lambda invocation after upload

This proves that attacker-controlled files can be uploaded and processed by the backend.

Part 6) Fix Strategy / Probable Mitigation

The vulnerability can be mitigated by:

Restricting S3 bucket write access
Enforcing least-privilege access control
Preventing unauthorized uploads
Validating file structure and origin before processing
Part 7) Code / Config Changes

A restrictive S3 bucket policy was applied:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "RestrictPutObject",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::dvsa-receipts-bucket-446483465359-us-east-1/*"
    }
  ]
}

Additionally, S3 Block Public Access settings were enabled.

Part 8) Verification After Fix

The same upload attempt was repeated:

aws s3 cp test.raw \
s3://dvsa-receipts-bucket-446483465359-us-east-1/2026/04/15/test.raw

Result:

Upload failed with AccessDenied

This confirms that unauthorized uploads are no longer allowed.

Note:
The strict deny policy also prevented the application from generating new receipts, indicating that the fix was too restrictive.